---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Subscribe from "../components/Subscribe.astro";
import { SITE, SERIES, SERIES_IDS } from "../consts";
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="Who writes this, what it covers, and the standard every post is held to."
>
  <h1>About</h1>

  <p>
    I write as <strong>{SITE.author}</strong>. I am a control engineer working
    on humanoid robots in China, and this site is where I write about the part
    of that work that belongs to everyone: the methods, the tooling, and what
    the industry looks like from the inside.
  </p>
  <p>
    In email, Gavin is fine.
  </p>

  <h2>What you will find here</h2>
  <ul>
    {
      SERIES_IDS.map((id) => (
        <li style="margin-bottom:.6rem">
          <a href={`/series/${id}/`}>{SERIES[id].name}</a> — {SERIES[id].blurb}
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <h2>The standard</h2>
  <p>
    The topics here are wide — supply chains, agent tooling, control theory.
    What holds them together is not the subject but the method, and it is worth
    stating plainly so you can hold me to it:
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    Primary sources. Real numbers. And an explicit statement of where the
    uncertainty is.
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    That last one matters most. When a figure comes from a public catalogue
    rather than something I measured, I will say so. When I am extrapolating, I
    will say that too. A reader who cannot tell the difference between what I
    know and what I am guessing has no reason to trust either.
  </p>
  <p>
    Nothing here comes from my employer. Where a post touches my day job, the
    method is mine and the data is from simulation, public URDFs, published
    specifications, or hardware I bought myself.
  </p>

  <h2>Get in touch</h2>
  <p>
    Email is best: <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a>. I read
    everything, and I reply to anything that is not a pitch.
  </p>
  <p>
    I take a small amount of consulting work — usually joint-level control and
    system identification, or helping teams outside China understand the
    actuator supply chain here. If that is why you are reading, say so in the
    first line and I will tell you quickly whether I can help.
  </p>

  <h2>Following along</h2>
  <p>
    Everything published here is available as <a href="/rss.xml">a feed</a>, or
    <a href="/posts/rss.xml">long-form only</a> if the short posts are noise to
    you. If you would rather have the essays by email, the form below does that
    and nothing else.
  </p>

  <Subscribe variant="inline" />
</Base>
